WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:04.320
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.030
Hope you're all having a good day.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.150
So right now I'm here in Munich

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.278
at our office, at Building Radar.

00:00:10.278 --> 00:00:12.140
So some of you probably already know,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.190
I currently work in a role
as a Chief Revenue Officer

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:16.562
at this mature startup.

00:00:16.562 --> 00:00:19.830
We are a company in the PropTech sector.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.480
I recently also made a video

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.181
about what it's like to work here.

00:00:23.181 --> 00:00:25.260
I will link it somewhere up here,

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.928
if you have not seen it yet.

00:00:26.928 --> 00:00:29.960
And for today's video,
I want to talk about

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.220
a topic that is meeting behavior,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.220
because I know that this
is a topic that many people

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.770
do struggle with, especially juniors,

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.960
and I've seen there's more
fear at Building Radar,

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.450
but also in my previous
roles in consulting.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.040
And very specifically, often
people are in a meeting

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.000
and wonder what shall I say, right?

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.970
You feel shy, it's a bit
difficult to add value,

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.360
to contribute.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.640
What is it that you can say
as a junior in a meeting

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.370
to really make a good impression,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.370
make a good impression about yourself?

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.500
And specifically, I would
love to share three tips

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.888
for you here today.

00:01:05.888 --> 00:01:09.150
So let's dig into it without further ado.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:12.000
And the first tip is a
tip that a past partner

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.480
that I worked with, a
consultant once gave me,

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.180
and I thought that made a lot of sense,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.840
and for me, really made a difference,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.050
because I am as well,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.690
I would probably consider
myself rather to be an introvert

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:22.650
than an extrovert.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.170
And the partner told me, Heinrich,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:25.670
in the beginning of a meeting,

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.817
make it a point, make it a habit of yours,

00:01:27.817 --> 00:01:32.120
that in the first couple
of minutes of a meeting

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.337
you directly say something,
even if it's something small,

00:01:35.337 --> 00:01:38.260
even if it's something
that's not that important,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.410
say something in the beginning.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:41.784
Because as a matter of fact,

00:01:41.784 --> 00:01:45.140
it's so much harder, much more difficult,

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.870
if you've been sitting in
a meeting for half an hour,

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.030
for an hour, or even later,

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.290
and all the time you have said nothing,

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.757
to then suddenly speak
up and say something.

00:01:56.757 --> 00:02:00.150
And the burden of that
will increase significantly

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.200
over time, the longer the meeting,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.200
the longer interaction goes
without you saying anything.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.293
So if you very early say something,

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:10.930
this will usually
significantly lower the burden

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.170
for you then to later on in the meeting,

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.860
say additional things.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.650
And it sounds like a minor tip,

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.920
but for me, it really made a difference.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.220
It was really, really important.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.530
So just try this out.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.220
I encourage you, and let
me know in the comments

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.720
whether this is making a
difference for you as well.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.850
Tip number two is about
proper preparation.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:34.140
So surely it will be much
easier for you to contribute

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.596
and to add value to meetings,

00:02:35.596 --> 00:02:38.670
if you prepare in the right way.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.791
And very specifically, what
I would recommend you to do

00:02:40.791 --> 00:02:43.380
is before you are in the meeting,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.440
before the meeting starts,

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.320
to take the meeting document,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.510
look at it,

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.860
and really think about
what could be questions

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.130
that you may be sensibly ask

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.360
at specific points of the document.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.549
Maybe to some other members, to clients,

00:02:57.549 --> 00:03:00.930
why it would insightful
to know more about that,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.276
or what would be helpful remarks

00:03:03.276 --> 00:03:06.201
that you can make on top of the document.

00:03:06.201 --> 00:03:10.190
And here it's not even necessary
to have something to say

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.046
every single minute,

00:03:11.046 --> 00:03:13.467
but if during the course of a meeting,

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:17.772
you say two or three smart
things, ask some smart questions.

00:03:17.772 --> 00:03:20.310
this will usually be more than enough

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:21.900
to make a good impression

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.300
and really to have, indeed,

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.810
added some value to the conversation.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.630
So preparation is key.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:28.880
Look about the document.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.290
Look over the document before the meeting.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.972
Write down a couple of points

00:03:32.972 --> 00:03:36.410
and this will, again,
help you also to be seen,

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:39.500
to be more visible in the conversations.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.410
The third tip is about
working in the correct

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.340
and sensible way with data,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.960
because it's just a fact
that you as a junior

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.590
will in most cases be most responsible

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:50.980
for the data analysis,

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.590
for the numbers that you crunch,

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.560
the Excel models that you build.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.150
And at least it would be usual
that then in the documents

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.620
that you have there often
will be some charts,

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.108
some data visualization

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:06.378
that was used to just put these
numbers into the document,

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:07.900
and here be ready

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.095
and potentially also
think about it in advance,

00:04:10.095 --> 00:04:12.968
what could be key points of information

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:16.240
that you would like to share on the data?

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.420
What would be really
insightful for you to add?

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:23.050
Things that are maybe not
directly visible on the chart

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.170
What are likely questions
that people will ask you

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.900
when they look at this data?

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.820
This is usually where you as a junior

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.026
can add the most significant value,

00:04:32.026 --> 00:04:35.480
because it's just the fact that
often even the project lead,

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.177
and for sure also the partners,

00:04:37.177 --> 00:04:41.690
they are not into that level
of detail into all the analysis

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.700
that you do in most situations
like this, as the associate,

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.440
as the consultant, as the analyst,

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.540
to prepare these analysis.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.870
No one is better informed
about what you actually did.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.300
No one knows the models, the calculations

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.920
to that level of detail as
you do, because you made them.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.130
So this is really your point,
your position of strength.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.010
This is where you shine,

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.670
where you have the expertise.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.716
And here regularly
project leads and partners

00:05:06.716 --> 00:05:09.810
will also expect and depend on you,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.060
that if there is a
question in that regard,

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.480
that you will be able to answer,
that you can pick this up.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.640
And to the contrary,

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.360
this will actually often be
also a bad point of feedback.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.100
If there are questions on
this topic, you sit there,

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.700
you are not adding anything,

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.320
even though you would be the
one who would be responsible

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.440
for this piece of work.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.390
So be ready, be prepared.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:32.223
Think about that.

00:05:32.223 --> 00:05:33.322
What are likely questions?

00:05:33.322 --> 00:05:36.840
How can you answer them in
a precise and concise way?

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.229
This is from my experience,
from my perspective,

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:41.850
one of the most direct ways
where you can add value

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.360
in these meetings as a junior.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.150
So I hope these tips made
sense and were helpful to you.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:48.960
I would also love to hear
from you from your experience,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:51.140
